| 1 | K | Let's pretend together. *How do we go into a make-believe place together, and how do we each add to it?* | 18 | th-k-imagine; th-k-show | TH:Cr1.1.Ka; TH:Cr2.1.Kb; TH:Cr3.1.Ka; TH:Cr2.1.Ka; TH:Re9.1.Ka | Creating (Cr1, Cr2, Cr3); responding through taking part (Re9). Works: kamishibai, Japanese paper theater, a teller sliding picture cards through a small wooden stage, a street storytelling form of 1930s Japan, described from a museum's or a university library's kamishibai collection page (the unit plan records the page and its date) and shown with a Curio Class original kamishibai story whose imagined place the class then enters; Polyglot Theatre (Melbourne, Australia), a company that makes theater young children play inside, such as its paper-and-cardboard play worlds, described and linked to the company's own pages (polyglot.org.au; the unit plan records the work and whether it still tours, as of its date) | TH:Cr1.1.Ka: with the teacher in role as a guide, invent and enter an imagined place (a forest, a seabed, a moon) in pretend play, starting from the what-if list. TH:Cr2.1.Kb: share an original idea in the play ("what if there is a cave"). TH:Cr3.1.Ka: ask and answer questions in the play, with the teacher in role asking ("Who are you? What do you see?") and the children asking back. TH:Cr2.1.Ka: work with classmates and add to the play. TH:Re9.1.Ka: take an active part with others, by the group agreements (take turns, add on, freeze on the signal). Technique: freeze and melt on a signal, and walking as someone in a place (tiptoe on ice, wading in mud), with the step card. Words: pretend, imagine, place, idea, question, together, turn, join, add. Showing: the class's imagined place shown to a partner class, each group of five entering it in turn. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 16, teacher's-table day 17), id by id: TH:Cr1.1.Ka, a guide look-for on the showing: the child enters the place and acts in it (walks, looks, touches as if it is there); TH:Cr2.1.Ka, a guide look-for on the showing: the child adds an action to what a classmate began; TH:Re9.1.Ka, a guide look-for on the showing: an active turn, by the agreements; TH:Cr2.1.Kb, a talk item: the child tells one idea of their own for a new place card the teacher shows; TH:Cr3.1.Ka, a talk item: the teacher in role asks two questions about the new place and the child answers one and asks one back. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: building a plot in order (grade 1), dialogue (grade 2). Tools: the what-if card, the agreements card, step cards, the kamishibai story cards, all Curio Class's own. Free path: a cardboard box frame for the kamishibai cards, the class's own chairs and floor. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First because every later unit needs the clear-floor routine, the agreements and the freeze signal (the what-if-list and group-agreements models start here). Pretending in a place opens the band so a child plays before being asked to use voice, props or a tale. Longest kindergarten unit with unit 2: five ids and the room's routines learned for the first time | Candidate: the bar's criterion 9 on the set-up and close minutes when desks must be moved in a 40-minute session | Hazards and controls: desks and chairs to the walls before play, bags off the floor; bare feet or soft soles; walking only in the play, no running, no lifts, no falls; freeze signal practiced before any movement. A child may watch first and join later (th-k-show note); no one is made to play a role. Kamishibai: the sensitivity agent reads the collection page first; the class makes its own story cards, not copies of a teller's. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | K | Voices and sounds. *How do our voices and sounds tell a story, and how do we feel about the characters?* | 18 | th-k-actor; th-k-notice | TH:Pr5.1.Ka; TH:Pr6.1.Ka; TH:Re7.1.Ka; TH:Re8.1.Ka | Performing (Pr5, Pr6); responding (Re7, Re8). Works: The Three Billy Goats Gruff (Norway, collected by Peter Christen AsbjA,rnsen and JA,rgen Moe in the 1840s; George Webbe Dasent's English telling in Popular Tales from the Norse, 1859, public domain), retold by Curio Class: three goat voices, a troll's voice and the trip-trap of hooves; the kamishibai form from unit 1, revisited: how one teller makes every voice; a child-friendly show, a Curio Class original sound story performed live by the teacher | TH:Pr5.1.Ka: see that sound and the voice are what pretend play is made of: the same story told once flat and once with voices and sounds, and the children say which helps. TH:Pr6.1.Ka: use the voice and sounds in a guided story (the wind, a door, an animal, a character's voice), with the teacher leading. TH:Re7.1.Ka: share how you feel about a character, by a thumb, a face or a word. TH:Re8.1.Ka: name what you liked best in the play or the teacher's show. Technique: the voice warm-up (hum, big and small, high and low, fast and slow) and a character voice for each goat, with the step card. The audience journal begins: a folder of audience cards. Words: voice, sound, loud, quiet, feel, like, character, favorite. Showing: Billy Goats Gruff told by the class as a sound story (the class makes every sound and voice, the teacher's puppet is the troll) for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 34, teacher's-table day 35), id by id: TH:Pr6.1.Ka, a guide look-for on the showing: the child makes the sound or voice for their part at the cue; TH:Pr5.1.Ka, a talk item: the teacher tells a two-line story flat, then asks the child to add one sound and say why it is better; TH:Re7.1.Ka, a kept audience card from the showing day: a face circled for how the child felt about a