| 3 | K | Puppets and props. *How can a paper bag become a character, and how is a character like me?* | 14 | th-k-design; th-k-world | TH:Cr1.1.Kb; TH:Pr5.1.Kb; TH:Cn10.1.Ka; TH:Cn11.1.Ka | Creating (Cr1); performing (Pr5); connecting (Cn10, Cn11). Works: Chinese shadow puppetry, on UNESCO's list of the intangible cultural heritage of humanity since 2011, leather figures moved behind a lit screen, described from UNESCO's page (ich.unesco.org) and a Chinese museum's or cultural body's page; the Paper Bag Players (New York, founded 1958), a children's theater that made its shows from paper bags and cardboard, described from the company's own archive pages (the unit plan records whether it still performs, as of its date) | TH:Cr1.1.Kb: with help, make a paper-bag puppet, a prop or a costume piece from simple materials (a bag, a sock, card, a scarf). TH:Pr5.1.Kb: try out tech elements in a pretend-play scene: a flashlight beam on a sheet for a shadow, a shaker or a crinkle for a sound. TH:Cn10.1.Ka: find one way a story character is like you, or like a story friend or an invented child, marked the same. TH:Cn11.1.Ka: name a skill from another subject that turns up in the play (counting the steps to the cave, a song the puppets sing, drawing the map). The prop-sketch (a drawing before making) and the two-ties card begin. Technique: making a paper-bag puppet's face and moving its mouth to speech, and a hand shadow and a card shadow shape behind a sheet. Words: puppet, prop, costume, make, light, sound, like me, skill. Showing: a puppet and shadow scene for the class, each group using its puppets and one light or sound. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 50, teacher's-table day 51), id by id: TH:Cr1.1.Kb, a guide look-for on the showing: the child's own made puppet or prop used in the scene; TH:Pr5.1.Kb, a guide look-for on the showing: the child works the flashlight or the sound at the cue; TH:Cn10.1.Ka, a talk item: one way a character in the scene is like the child (or like a story friend or invented child); TH:Cn11.1.Ka, a talk item: one skill from another subject used in the play. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: planning a costume and naming tech (grade 1), scenery (grade 2). Tools: the prop-sketch sheet, the two-ties card, step cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: lunch bags, odd socks, a white sheet or a wall, a phone light held by the teacher in place of flashlights. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 2 because a puppet needs a character voice and a shadow scene needs sounds at a cue; before unit 4 because the tale is acted with these puppets and props. The prop-sketch and two-ties models start here | Candidate: a puppet form from Africa or the Americas beside the two works, from a museum's page | Hazards and controls: blunt-tip scissors and glue sticks; hands washed; no small parts (buttons, beads) a child could swallow; LED flashlights with screw-closed battery doors, no button or coin batteries, never shone into eyes; the sheet hung low and secure, nothing a child could pull down. Shadow puppetry: the sensitivity agent reads UNESCO's page and a Chinese cultural body's page first; children make their own animal and character shapes, not copies of the tradition's figures. "Like me" ties: a story friend or an invented child is offered beside the child's own life and marked the same. Health's steps if a child tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | K | Acting out a tale. *Who is in a story and where does it happen, and how is one tale different from another?* | 10 | th-k-story | TH:Pr4.1.Ka; TH:Re8.1.Kb; TH:Cn11.2.Ka; TH:Cn11.2.Kb | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re8); connecting (Cn11). Works: "How the Sky God's Stories Came to Be Called Spider Stories", the Anansi tale told by the Akan people of Ghana, from R. S. Rattray's Akan-Ashanti Folk-Tales (1930, public domain in the United States), retold by Curio Class, with the tale's own frame (the teller's opening and closing words) kept as the source gives it; Billy Goats Gruff from unit 2, revisited beside it: two tales told apart | TH:Pr4.1.Ka: with help, point out who the characters are and where the tale happens, on the story map. TH:Re8.1.Kb: name and describe the places in the tale (the sky, the forest, the bridge). TH:Cn11.2.Ka: tell two tales apart (who is in each, where each happens, how each ends). TH:Cn11.2.Kb: act out a brief tale with help: the Anansi tale, the class playing the animals Anansi meets and the teacher narrating, with the year's puppets and props. Technique: the story map in pictures, and acting a moment by the step card (freeze, show, say one line). Words: character, place, story, tale, act out, beginning. Showing: the Anansi tale acted for families at the year's end, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 61, teacher's-table day 62), id by id: TH:Cn11.2.Kb, a guide look-for on the showing: the child acts their part of the tale at its place in the story; TH:Pr4.1.Ka, a kept story map made on the last rehearsal day: the characters and the place drawn or pointed to, with the teacher's label; TH:Re8.1.Kb, a talk item: one place in the tale named and told about; TH:Cn11.2.Ka, a talk item: one way the Anansi tale and Billy Goats Gruff are different. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Day 64, after the respond day, holds the family showing. Out: why characters act (grade 1), causes and results (grade 2). Tools: the story map, the retelling, step cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: the year's paper-bag puppets and found props. Once-a-week path: 4 sessions | Last in the year because the tale is acted with the year's puppets, voices and agreements (units 1 to 3) and ends the year after the year-end check (week 27); the story-map model starts here, where it is first needed | Candidate: the Akan source confirmed against a Ghanaian or Akan-run telling of the tale | Nyame, the sky god of the tale, is the supreme being of Akan belief: the narrator (the teacher) tells his part and no child plays him; the sensitivity agent reads a Ghanaian or Akan cultural body's or a museum's page on Anansi stories first and checks the tale is told for all. The tale's trapped animals are tricked, not hurt, in the retelling; no child is tied or caught. No accents or costumes of Ghana; the animals are played by movement and voice. Family yes for showing outside class; a recorded or solo version is offered. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | 1 | Building a story together. *How do we build a story one step at a time, and who decides what happens next?* | 16 | th-g1-imagine; th-g1-show | TH:Cr1.1.1a; TH:Cr1.1.1c; TH:Cr2.1.1a; TH:Cr2.1.1b; TH:Cr3.1.1a; TH:Re9.1.1a | Creating (Cr1, Cr2, Cr3); responding through building on ideas (Re9). Works: The Enormous Turnip (Russia, from Aleksandr Afanasyev's folktale collections of the 1850s and 1860s, a public domain English telling the unit plan records), retold by Curio Class: a plot built one helper at a time; the kamishibai form from year K, revisited: how a teller builds a plot card by card; a story drama by Curio Class with a choice point the class votes on | Retrieval in day 2's opener: kindergarten entering a place (th-k-imagine) and the agreements (th-k-show). TH:Cr1.1.1a: suggest what a character might choose to do. TH:Cr1.1.1c: name ways gestures and movement can tell a tale (pulling, digging, falling back in a heap, shown as a slow-motion freeze, no real falling). TH:Cr2.1.1a: add the next step to a plot built in order (who comes next to pull the turnip). TH:Cr2.1.1b: join a group decision by a vote with hands or cards. TH:Cr3.1.1a: help change the plot (what if the mouse does not come). TH:Re9.1.1a: build on a classmate's idea ("yes, and"). Technique: the plot in steps on a story strip (first, next, then, last) and "yes, and" with the step card. Words: character, choice, gesture, movement, plot, step, decide, group, change, build. Showing: the class's changed Turnip, each group showing its own ending, for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 14, teacher's-table day 15), id by id: TH:Cr2.1.1a, a guide look-for on the showing: the child's step added in order; TH:Cr3.1.1a, a guide look-for on the showing: the group's changed ending, with the child's part in it; TH:Re9.1.1a, a guide look-for on the showing: the child adds on to a classmate's action or line; TH:Cr2.1.1b, a kept vote card from the last rehearsal day: the child's vote and the group's choice marked; TH:Cr1.1.1a, a talk item: what a character on a new scene card might choose to do; TH:Cr1.1.1c, a talk item: two gestures or movements that would tell a moment of the new scene. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: dialogue (grade 2). Tools: the story strip, vote cards, step cards, the retelling, Curio Class's own. Free path: the strip drawn on the board, hands for votes. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 1 because it reopens the drama room after the summer with the agreements, and moves from adding one idea to building and changing a plot. Both kindergarten models (what-if-list, group-agreements) are retrieved here, where they are first needed this year | Candidate: a cumulative tale from Africa, Asia or the Americas beside the Turnip, from a public domain telling | Hazards and controls: the heap at the end is a slow-motion freeze on the floor, no one falls or lands on anyone; pulling is mimed in a line with no hands on each other. Votes: a child who is outvoted still has a part; the teacher names that both ideas were good. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | 1 | Face, body, voice. *How can we show what a character feels without saying it, and what do we remember about the choices we made?* | 22 | th-g1-actor; th-g1-notice | TH:Pr4.1.1b; TH:Pr5.1.1a; TH:Cr3.1.1b; TH:Pr6.1.1a; TH:Re7.1.1a; TH:Re8.1.1a; TH:Re8.1.1c; TH:Re9.1.1c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5, Pr6); creating (Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8, Re9). Works: Marcel Marceau (France, 1923 to 2007), the mime whose character Bip showed feelings with face and body and no words, described from a public archive's page the unit plan records; the life-size horse puppet made by Adrian Kohler and Basil Jones of Handspring Puppet Company (Cape Town, South Africa; the unit plan records whether each is living, as of its date), a horse of cane and fabric that three puppeteers make breathe and flick its ears, described and linked to the company's own pages (handspringpuppet.co.za); a child-friendly show, a Curio Class original short scene the teacher performs | Retrieval in day 2's opener: kindergarten voices and sounds (th-k-actor) and saying how a character made you feel (th-k-notice). TH:Pr4.1.1b: show a character's traits and feelings through face, body, gesture and voice. TH:Pr5.1.1a: see that body movement is what guided drama is made of (the same moment frozen still, then moving). TH:Cr3.1.1b: spot how two characters' sounds and movements are alike and different. TH:Pr6.1.1a: show feelings through movement and gesture in guided drama. TH:Re7.1.1a: remember the choices made in a guided drama. TH:Re8.1.1a: explain what you preferred in the drama or the teacher's show and how it made you feel. TH:Re8.1.1c: compare your own feelings and choices with a character's, in words or pictures. TH:Re9.1.1c: compare what two characters went through. Technique: the feelings warm-up (a feeling shown in the face, then the shoulders, then the whole body, then the voice), and the mirror game with a partner, with the step card. Words: face, body, gesture, feeling, voice, move, remember, choice, prefer, compare. Showing: a "no words" scene, each group showing a character's feeling changing, for the class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 100, teacher's-table day 101), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.1b, a guide look-for on the showing: the character's feeling shown in face, body and gesture; TH:Pr6.1.1a, a guide look-for on the showing: the feeling changes by movement at the cue; TH:Re7.1.1a, a kept audience card from the showing day: one choice the child's group made, drawn; TH:Re8.1.1c, a kept audience card from the showing day: the child's own feeling beside a character's, in a drawing or a circled face, the same or different; TH:Pr5.1.1a, a talk item: the teacher freezes a moment, then moves it, and the child tells what the movement added; TH:Cr3.1.1b, a talk item: two characters the teacher shows, one way their movements are alike and one different; TH:Re8.1.1a, a kept audience card from the teacher's-table day, after the teacher's short original scene opens it: the part the child preferred, circled, and a face for how it felt; TH:Re9.1.1c, a kept audience card from the same scene: one thing each of two characters went through, drawn, with same or different marked. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: small differences in one character (grade 2). Tools: the feelings warm-up card, the mirror game card, audience cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: the floor, the voice and the audience card folder. Once-a-week path: 11 sessions | After unit 5 because a character with feelings is played inside a plot the class already knows how to build; the longest year 1 unit because it owns eight ids in two threads. Before unit 7 because a costume and a prop serve a character the child can already show | Candidate: a third work showing feeling without words by a maker from Asia or the Americas, described and linked | Hazards and controls: the mirror game is done at arm's length with no touching; clear floor and soft soles; no falls even when a character is sad or tired (a slow sink to sitting). Feelings: characters' feelings are the subject, and a child chooses whether to share a time they felt the same; a story character's or an invented child's feeling is offered beside it and marked the same. The horse puppet comes from a war story: the unit names only how the puppet is made and moved, never the war; the sensitivity agent reads the company's page first. Health's steps if a child tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 7 | 1 | One thing, many things. *How can a scarf become a river or a cape, and what do we bring to a drama from what we know?* | 12 | th-g1-design; th-g1-world | TH:Cr1.1.1b; TH:Pr5.1.1b; TH:Cr3.1.1c; TH:Re9.1.1b; TH:Cn10.1.1a; TH:Cn11.1.1a | Creating (Cr1, Cr3); performing (Pr5); responding (Re9); connecting (Cn10, Cn11). Works: mamulengo, the hand-puppet theater of northeast Brazil, carved wooden puppets in cloth clothes, registered by Brazil's national heritage institute as part of the popular puppet theater of the Northeast (as of its date), described from the institute's or a Brazilian museum's page; bunraku (Japan, on UNESCO's intangible heritage list since 2008), where three puppeteers in view move one puppet with its costume and props, described from UNESCO's page | Retrieval in day 2's opener: kindergarten puppets and props (th-k-design) and "like me" (th-k-world). TH:Cr1.1.1b: with classmates, plan a costume piece and a prop for a guided drama, on the prop-sketch. TH:Pr5.1.1b: with help, name tech elements that could help the drama (a light, a sound, a sheet for the sky). TH:Cr3.1.1c: with others, imagine many ways one everyday object can stand for something else (a scarf as a river, a cape, a baby). TH:Re9.1.1b: name the props and costumes each child could bring into the drama, from the class box first (no child must bring anything from home). TH:Cn10.1.1a: name a character's feelings and tie them to your own life, or to a story friend's or an invented child's, marked the same. TH:Cn11.1.1a: bring what you know from another subject or art into the drama (a song from music, counting coins at a pretend market). Technique: the object game (pass one object round the circle, each child uses it as something new) and the costume piece planned before it is made, with the step card. Words: costume, prop, plan, stand for, tech, object, feeling, life, know. Showing: a market or a journey drama with planned props and one named light or sound, for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 114, teacher's-table day 115), id by id: TH:Cr3.1.1c, a guide look-for on the showing: the child's object used as at least two things in the drama; TH:Cr1.1.1b, a kept prop-sketch made on the last making day: the planned costume piece or prop, drawn and labeled with the teacher; TH:Re9.1.1b, a kept prop-sketch item: the list of props and costumes the group chose from the class box, the child's own marked; TH:Pr5.1.1b, a kept prop-sketch item: one light or sound picture circled that could help the drama, with what it would do told to the teacher and noted; TH:Cn10.1.1a, a talk item: a character's feeling named and tied to a time in the child's life, or to a story friend or an invented child; TH:Cn11.1.1a, a talk item: one thing from another subject the child brought into the drama. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: planning a set and trying effects (grade 2). Tools: the prop-sketch sheet, the two-ties card, the object game card, Curio Class's own. Free path: the class box of found objects (scarves, boxes, short card tubes; no sticks). Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | After unit 6 because a costume and a prop serve a character whose feelings the child can show, and the feeling ties to the child's life grow from comparing self and character. Before unit 8 because the scene from a fiction source uses planned props | Candidate: a third object-theater work by a living maker from Africa or Asia, described and linked | Hazards and controls: no stick, rod or tube longer than a forearm; no weapons, real, toy or mimed, and no contact fights; a scarf or a card tube stands for tools, rivers and capes only; objects checked by the teacher before use; no cords. Bringing props: the class box covers every child; "what could you bring" is answered from the box or from pretend, so no family has to supply anything. Mamulengo and bunraku: the sensitivity agent reads the Brazilian heritage page and UNESCO's page first; mamulengo's adult comic scenes are not shown; children make their own puppets, not copies. Feeling ties: an invented option is offered beside the child's own. Health's steps if a child tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 8 | 1 | Stories from our community. *Why do characters do what they do, and how are the stories told around us alike and different?* | 10 | th-g1-story | TH:Pr4.1.1a; TH:Re8.1.1b; TH:Cn11.2.1a; TH:Cn11.2.1b | Performing (Pr4); responding (Re8); connecting (Cn11). Works: Aesop's The Lion and the Mouse (ancient Greece; George Fyler Townsend's English telling, 1867, public domain), retold by Curio Class, the fiction source for the scene; stories told in the class's own community: a story told by a visiting family member, a librarian or a local storyteller by the school's choice (a living teller, credited by name only with their yes), or a Curio Class set of short stories told in one town if no visitor comes | Retrieval in day 2's opener: kindergarten who and where in a tale (th-k-story). TH:Pr4.1.1a: describe what a story character does and says. TH:Re8.1.1b: name the causes of a character's actions (why the mouse frees the lion). TH:Cn11.2.1a: notice how stories told in your own community are alike and unlike (two stories heard this unit, side by side on the story map). TH:Cn11.2.1b: together, make a short scene from a fiction source (The Lion and the Mouse, the net mimed). Technique: the story map with a "because" arrow, and a scene in three freezes (start, problem, end) joined by movement. Words: cause, action, scene, community, fiction, alike, because. Showing: The Lion and the Mouse acted for families at the year's end, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 125, teacher's-table day 126), id by id: TH:Cn11.2.1b, a guide look-for on the showing: the group's three-freeze scene from the fable, the child's part in it; TH:Pr4.1.1a, a talk item: what a character in a new short story the teacher tells does and says; TH:Re8.1.1b, a kept story map made on the last rehearsal day: the "because" arrow from an action to its cause, drawn with the teacher's label; TH:Cn11.2.1a, a talk item: one way two community stories heard in the unit are alike and one way they differ. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Day 128, after the respond day, holds the family showing. Out: stories from several cultures, a scene from nonfiction (grade 2). Tools: the story map, the retelling, step cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: the year's props and puppets. Once-a-week path: 4 sessions | Last in grade 1 because the scene uses the year's plot-building, feelings and props (units 5 to 7) and ends the year after the year-end check (week 27); the story-map model returns here from year K | Candidate: the community storyteller visit planned with the school's own rules for visitors | Community stories: a family's story is told only by the family's choice, and the Curio Class set of short stories stands in for any child whose family does not share one; no child is asked to tell their own family's story. The lion in the net is mimed; no child is tied. Family yes for showing outside class; a recorded or solo version is offered. Health's steps if a child tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 9 | 2 | Words for the characters. *How do we make up what characters say, and how do we decide together where a story goes?* | 16 | th-g2-imagine; th-g2-show | TH:Cr1.1.2a; TH:Cr1.1.2c; TH:Cr2.1.2a; TH:Cr2.1.2b; TH:Cr3.1.2a; TH:Pr6.1.2a; TH:Re9.1.2a | Creating (Cr1, Cr2, Cr3); performing (Pr6); responding through working together (Re9). Works: The Monkey and the Crocodile, from the Panchatantra (India; Arthur W. Ryder's English telling, 1925, public domain in the United States), retold by Curio Class: a talk between two characters on a river; the Anansi tale from year K, revisited: how the teller gives each animal its words | Retrieval in day 2's opener: a plot built in order (th-g1-imagine) and group decisions (th-g1-show). TH:Cr1.1.2a: suggest new plot and story details (what the crocodile's wife wants, what is on the riverbank). TH:Cr1.1.2c: name ways the voice and sounds can tell the tale (the river, the monkey's fast voice, the crocodile's slow one). TH:Cr2.1.2a: with classmates, make up lines of dialogue that matter to the story. TH:Cr2.1.2b: share ideas and decide together how the story goes on. TH:Cr3.1.2a: help change some of the lines after trying them. TH:Pr6.1.2a: pitch in on the group drama, then share it with the class, for the first time. TH:Re9.1.2a: work together on a scene. Technique: a line said three ways (fast, slow, whisper) and the "line, reply" pair, with the step card. Words: detail, voice, sound, dialogue, plot, invent, scene, share, together, audience. Showing: each group's Monkey and Crocodile scene with its own lines, shared with the class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 142, teacher's-table day 143), id by id: TH:Pr6.1.2a, a guide look-for on the showing: the child takes part and the scene is shared with the class; TH:Re9.1.2a, a guide look-for on the showing: the group plays the scene together, each child in their place; TH:Cr2.1.2a, a guide look-for on the showing: a line of dialogue the child made up, said in the scene; TH:Cr3.1.2a, a kept scene card from the last rehearsal day: a line the group changed, with the old and new line told to the teacher and noted; TH:Cr2.1.2b, a kept vote card from the last rehearsal day: the idea the child shared and what the group decided; TH:Cr1.1.2a, a talk item: two new details for a scene card the child has not seen; TH:Cr1.1.2c, a talk item: one way the voice or a sound would tell a moment of the new scene. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a method for finding ideas, whole characters by improvisation (grade 3). Tools: scene cards, vote cards, step cards, the retelling, Curio Class's own. Free path: lines told aloud and kept by the teacher on the board. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 2 because it reopens the drama room and moves from building a plot to making up its words; the scene shared with classmates for the first time sits here because the next three units each share a scene | Candidate: a dialogue tale from the Americas or Oceania beside the Panchatantra tale, from a public domain telling | The crocodile wants the monkey's heart to eat: the retelling keeps the trick (the monkey says he left his heart in the tree) and leaves out any harm; no child plays being eaten. The Panchatantra is a collection of teaching tales from India; the sensitivity agent reads an Indian cultural body's or a museum's page first. No accents. A shy child may be the river's sound or share a recorded line (th-g2-show note). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 10 | 2 | Small changes, big differences. *How do small changes in voice and movement show who a character is, and when do people in a drama decide things?* | 16 | th-g2-actor; th-g2-notice | TH:Pr4.1.2b; TH:Pr5.1.2a; TH:Cr3.1.2b; TH:Re7.1.2a; TH:Re8.1.2a; TH:Re8.1.2c; TH:Re9.1.2c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); creating (Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8, Re9). Works: Peking opera (China, on UNESCO's intangible heritage list since 2010), where performers show a character by a set walk, a hand gesture and a voice, studied by watching a description and still pictures from UNESCO's page and a Chinese cultural body's page (living performers today); Marcel Marceau's Bip from unit 6, revisited: one small change of face and shoulders; a Curio Class original short scene the teacher performs, with three decision moments | Retrieval in day 2's opener: face, body, gesture and voice (th-g1-actor) and comparing self with a character (th-g1-notice). TH:Pr4.1.2b: change how you sound and move to show small differences in one character (the same giant tired, proud, then scared). TH:Pr5.1.2a: show how body, mind and voice work together (think the thought, then move, then speak). TH:Cr3.1.2b: use and adjust movements and sounds as you go, after a partner's describe, ask, suggest. TH:Re7.1.2a: notice the moments when the people in a drama decide things. TH:Re8.1.2a: explain how your own likes and feelings shape how you react as an audience member. TH:Re8.1.2c: compare other people's feelings and choices with a character's, in words or pictures. TH:Re9.1.2c: tell how characters handle the problems they face. Technique: the one-character, three-ways drill (a walk, a hand, a voice changed one at a time), with the step card. Words: sound, move, adjust, mind, subtle, character, decide, reaction, compare, problem. Showing: one character in three moods, each group showing the changes, for the class. End-of-unit evidence (showing day 158, teacher's-table day 159), id by id: TH:Pr4.1.2b, a guide look-for on the showing: the character's three moods shown by small changes of voice and movement; TH:Cr3.1.2b, a guide look-for on the showing: the change the child made after the critique, kept in the scene; TH:Re7.1.2a, a kept audience card from the showing day: one moment a character decided something, drawn or marked on the scene strip; TH:Re8.1.2c, a kept audience card from the showing day: a classmate's feeling about a character beside the character's own, circled faces with a word; TH:Pr5.1.2a, a talk item: the child shows a character thinking, then moving, then speaking, and tells which came first; TH:Re8.1.2a, a talk item on the teacher's original scene, which opens the teacher's-table day: why the child reacted to it as they did, from their own likes and feelings; TH:Re9.1.2c, a kept audience card from the same scene: how a character handled its problem, drawn with a word. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: sets of exercises for body, voice and thinking (grade 3), judging a work (grade 3). Tools: the drill card, the scene strip, audience cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: the floor and the audience card folder. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 9 because a character's small changes are shown in a scene the class can now share with lines; before unit 11 because a costume piece that shows a character needs the character already shown in the body. Same-year lean on ELA (SL.2.4) with its "if later" line, Cross-subject | Candidate: a third work, by a living maker from Africa or the Americas, showing a character through movement, described and linked | Peking opera: studied by watching and describing only; no child paints a face, wears a costume of the form or imitates its voice; the sensitivity agent reads UNESCO's page and a Chinese cultural body's page first. Hazards and controls: clear floor, a giant's stomp is a slow, heavy walk, never a jump; no falls when scared (a freeze). Reactions: a child's own likes are their own and never marked down