ntence describing a classmate's toy preference, from what the interview gathered (W.K.2). Foundations block: print concepts on the class poem and big book; letters a, t, h, p, n, c, m, r named, formed and sounded (Skills Block cycles 1 to 4), with the sound-spelling-map started; rhymes and spoken syllables; the first high-frequency words (I, the, in), each mapped. Continued in unit 2: ek-letter-names and ek-letter-sounds to all 26 letters, ek-early-phonology into onset and rime, ek-high-frequency two or three words a cycle all year. | The base text's module 1, six weeks, built for launching routines: the read-aloud routine, book handling and the talk protocols are used every later day, so they come first. Letters and sounds start in week 1 because every foundations skill after rests on them. Toys are a topic every child can talk about while routines are learned. The opinion piece sits here because the base text teaches and first checks W.K.1 here, on the classroom toys the children prefer, and the kindergarten standard asks only a topic and an opinion, so it needs no earlier model; it starts claim-evidence-reasoning, which unit 4's author reasons build on. The informative piece needs only the main-idea-details model introduced in this unit. | Candidate: the base text's module 1 unit assessments, adapted to our texts (key details and pictures with partner talk; writing about the classroom toys we prefer, W.K.1; the partner interview; the classmate's toy preference piece, W.K.2) and the Skills Block cycle 1 to 4 letter and sound checks; checked in the unit plan. | Homes have different toys: talk is about classroom toys, and no child is asked what they own. The partner interview is about a classroom toy only. Sharing stories show both children's feelings; no real child's conflict is replayed. Classmates' names on the class poem stay in the room. |
