rds a cycle. Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.K.1a, L.K.2c, RF.K.2a, RF.K.2b, RF.K.2c (unit 1); L.K.2d (unit 3). | The base text's module 2. Weather information comes first in the unit (days 35 to 48): the class observes weather every day, so description and the journal have fresh material. Story elements follow from day 49, because retelling and the weather story both use the story-map. Phonemes come after unit 1's rhymes and syllables (larger sound units first) and before unit 3's decoding, which needs blending. Sentences and grammar in talk come now because the weather story is the first piece written in sentences. | Candidate: the base text's module 2 unit assessments, adapted (independent writing about weather; story elements in a weather story; a small-group discussion reflecting on the weather story) and the Skills Block cycle 5 to 11 checks; checked in the unit plan. | A storm or flood story may worry a child who has lived through one: stories end safe, no disaster photographs, and a quiet way out of the talk. Clothes for the weather: no child is asked what clothes they have at home. Describing family or home (ek-describe-aloud): any family is accepted, "a person you know" is offered, no private details are pressed. The digital story: no child account, no name or photograph posted outside the class. Weather is described in words and pictures only, no temperature numbers (kindergarten math counts to 100 but reads no thermometer). |
