he group invented, clear to the audience; TH:Cr1.1.3c, a guide look-for on the showing: the agreed way the student's character moves and speaks; TH:Cr3.1.3a, a guide look-for on the showing: the scene fits the two-minute limit and the marked space; TH:Pr6.1.3a, the kept rehearsal-log page from the last rehearsal day, one line alone and one with the group; TH:Cr2.1.3a, the kept idea sheet: the method the student tried and the idea it gave; TH:Cr2.1.3b, a written item: from two ideas for the unseen scene, the one the student would keep and why it makes the scene stronger. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: given circumstances (grade 4), staging problems (grade 6). Tools: scenario cards, step cards, the idea sheet, the rehearsal-log page, Curio Class's own. Free path: chairs, a scarf, the voice. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 3 because every later unit improvises and rehearses in groups: the rehearsal-log model starts here and the what-if list and group agreements are retrieved where they are first needed this year. Commedia's posted outline makes improvising inside a limit concrete before the actor's tools are built in unit 2 | Candidate: a third improvising tradition from Africa or the Americas, described and linked | Commedia used half-masks and stock types, some tied to regions: students play a character's want and walk, never an accent, a regional type or a mask of a real group; masks are studied in pictures only. Hazards and controls: a clear floor, chairs to the walls; freezes held still, no falls; the passed object checked by the teacher. A student who does not want to perform takes the timer job or the "Where" objects and is marked on the idea sheet and log. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
