5: ## The course in brief
6: 
7: - **Units.** 12: four in each year. Every year has the same four-unit shape, so a technique and a thread come back
8:   one step harder a year later in the same place: an inventing-together unit (improvising and group rehearsal), an
9:   actor's tools unit (body and voice exercises and the watching eye), a design and community unit (sets, costumes,
10:   lights and sound, and the drama's ties to community and culture), and a story and judging unit that ends the year
11:   with a play for families. Each unit joins two progression threads, one of creating or performing and one of
12:   responding or connecting, so every unit makes, performs, studies another artist's work and shows (bar criteria 4
13:   and 5). Grades 3 to 5 theater has eight rows a year; judging becomes its own thread in grade 3 and joins the story
14:   unit.
15: - **Days.** 192: 64 a year for three years. Each year: 60 days in its four units, 2 course-level check days (one
16:   mid-year, one year-end) and 2 respond days, one after each check. One session of 40 minutes, two sessions a week,
17:   32 weeks a year (STANDARD.md 3.4: a K to 8 arts year is 64 course days; it never needs a daily period, bar
18:   criterion 9). The 40 minutes hold the set-up routine (about 3 minutes: chairs and bags to the walls, the clear
19:   floor checked, the group agreements card shown), the warm-up (5), the teaching, improvising and rehearsing, and the
20:   close (about 4: chairs back, props to the class box, the day's evidence collected); a making session with card,
21:   tape and fabric takes 6 for clean-up. A readiness or retrieval task takes ten minutes. Each unit's days include its
22:   end-of-unit assessment (two check days: a showing day and a written day; no unit is over 25 days, so none has a
23:   mid-unit assessment) and one respond day after it. Unit counts follow the rows' load: the design and community unit
24:   owns seven ids and two products (a design and a community scene), so it is longest in year 3, where design sketches
25:   that suit a setting and tech compared across works are first met; the actor's tools unit is longest in year 5,
26:   where responses are written and defended for the first time and feeling is traced to breath and pitch; the story
27:   and judging unit is shortest (five ids) in years 3 and 5 and two days longer in year 4, which compares two
28:   cultures and two eras besides rewriting an ending. These are course days: the school calendar
29:   (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md) spreads them two a week, so course day n of a year falls in that year's week n / 2,
30:   rounded up. National time figure (STANDARD.md 3.4): none is stated, because the theater association gives no
31:   minutes on a page read so far (For the lead item 3); the course gives 80 minutes a week, and 40 on the once-a-week
32:   path. The course is written so a classroom teacher can teach it; a school with a drama teacher uses that teacher.
33: - **The once-a-week path.** A school that meets once a week keeps every unit and every id in 32 sessions a year by
34:   merging the days each unit marks (STANDARD.md 3.4). Each unit keeps, in this order and no fewer: the demonstration
35:   with a step card, opened by the look or listen at a work that carries the unit's response and its connecting
36:   sentence; one rehearsal session with the critique; the showing (the check); one respond session, whose first 15
37:   minutes hold the written items before the second try. Fluency days fold into the openers. Sessions on that path:
38:   year 3, units of 8, 8, 8 and 4; year 4, 8, 8, 7 and 5; year 5, 8, 9, 7 and 4; each year's two checks keep 1 check
39:   session and 1 respond session. Check sessions fall in the same weeks as on the full path (year 3 weeks 17 and 27,
40:   year 4 weeks 17 and 26, year 5 weeks 18 and 27); each respond session falls one week later. A merged day never
41:   drops a critique, a revision or a respond day; the scope and sequence marks which days merge.
42: - **Standard.** National Core Arts Standards, 2014, theater performance standards, by id only, each named with its
43:   line in data/standards/ncas-performance-standards-words.txt, never quoted (STANDARD.md 2). The band-year rule binds
44:   (STANDARD.md 3.1): year 3 claims only the 24 grade 3 ids (`.3a`, `.3b`, `.3c`), year 4 only the 24 grade 4 ids,
45:   year 5 only the 24 grade 5 ids: 72 in all, the count in STANDARD.md 3.2, every one taught and assessed in its own
46:   year. A year revisits the year before's ids as practice that returns, never as a claim. Every year touches all
47:   four artistic processes (creating, performing, responding, connecting; bar criterion 1), and each unit touches at
48:   least two. In the Units table the C3 column is headed "Processes, anchors and works": the artistic processes and
49:   anchor standards the unit works on, and the works it studies. Check set: each unit plan checks itself against the
