120:   question. Every year 4 and year 5 unit assumes the year before's row in the same thread (one grade back) and opens
121:   the same way, with its retrieval task inside day 2's opener. The count starts from the band's order (3, then 4,
122:   then 5). In a mixed class running the years in turn, a student may meet year 5 before year 4: each retrieval task
123:   then uses its ten-minute reteach (APPROACH "Readiness and the baseline look"). The Seams tables give each row's last
124:   use.
125: - **Course-level checks.** Grades K to 8 after-core checks avoid the weeks where docs/courses/CALENDAR.md shows a core
126:   check for the grade (grade 3: 9, 12, 14, 16, 20, 23, 25, 28, 30 to 32; grade 4: 8 to 10, 12, 16, 19, 21, 23, 27,
127:   30 to 32; grade 5: 10, 11, 16, 17, 21, 22, 29 to 32). Weeks chosen by the units' load, not to dodge other
128:   after-core courses (the lead's calendar pass does that): mid-year in week 17 in years 3 and 4 (course days 33 and
129:   34 of the year) and week 18 in year 5 (days 35 and 36); year-end in week 27 in years 3 and 5 (days 53 and 54) and
130:   week 26 in year 4 (days 51 and 52). Weeks 30 to 32 hold core year-end checks in all three grades, so the year-end
131:   check comes before the story and judging unit (CALENDAR.md stagger tool c), and that unit's showing to families, in
132:   weeks 28 to 32 (27 to 32 in year 4), is its own end-of-unit assessment.
133: - **Leave-out.** A school or family may leave out a unit or a work for its community's reasons (STANDARD.md 5); each
134:   unit stands alone. Every unit of this course uses play-acting, which STANDARD.md 5 names as a reason a community may
135:   leave a unit out; a school that leaves out play-acting leaves out the course, and the course page says so plainly.
136:   Units that study a cultural or ceremonial tradition, named on the course page: unit 2 (Japanese kyogen), unit 4
137:   (the Mande griots and the Sosso-Bala of Guinea, studied by hearing and looking only), unit 5 (Indonesian wayang,
138:   studied by looking only), unit 7 (a Sicangu Lakota playwright's work on Native characters), unit 8 (ancient Greek
139:   theater, made for a festival of a god; the Turkish meddah), unit 10 (Korean pansori), unit 11 (Mexican American
140:   farmworker theater; Kenyan community theater) and unit 12 (a Zuni story, studied by reading and comparing only).
141:   Swapping a work keeps the unit's ids met; leaving a unit out means the course page and the unit's family note name
142:   the ids not claimed that year.
143: - **The ordinary room.** A class of 30 with a clear floor (desks pushed back or a hall), chairs, card, tape, paper,
144:   fabric scraps, found objects, pencils and the voice; nothing must be bought, and every unit names a free stand-in
145:   (STANDARD.md 3.4, APPROACH "Supplies"). One student at home: the parent is the audience and the critique partner, a
146:   group scene has a doubled version (parent and student, or the student playing two characters with two objects), and
147:   the showing is to the family or a recorded take. The teacher needs no drama training: each unit's teacher page says
148:   what to look and listen for, in plain words.
149: - **Supplies and cost.** Provisional cost per unit for a class of 30 (consumables, reusable items counted once,
150:   printing of step cards, scene cards, response cards and audience cards), all optional beside the free path: year 3
151:   $5, $5, $20 (card, tape and fabric scraps for costume pieces and set models), $5 ($35); year 4 $5, $5, $25 (six
152:   small LED flashlights, reusable, and colored film), $5 ($40); year 5 $5, $5, $15 (card for cue sheets and set
153:   models; the flashlights reused), $5 ($30). Planning figures, not quotes; any ceiling is under For the lead.
154: - **Devices.** None per student. One teacher screen may show a work's page, a still or a public domain film clip;
155:   every day runs from the printed step card, scene card and description without it. A school device may record a
156:   group the teacher cannot watch live (Scored evidence). No owned id needs technology: TH:Re9.1.3b, TH:Re9.1.4b and
157:   TH:Re9.1.5b study tech elements in works, which a paper-path student meets through the work card's description and
158:   still pictures in full. Kept work stays on paper or on the school's or family's device (APPROACH "Kept work").
159: - **Paper path.** Every day runs on the floor, the voice, paper, card, objects and printed cards; any Curio Class
160:   tool a unit plan adds (a stage-directions practice tool, a cue-sheet builder, a sound-effects board) has a printed
161:   version that does the same job and needs no student account.
162: - **Reading load.** A student reads at most one short card alone (APPROACH "Supports"): a scene card, a fact card or
163:   a response card of a few lines; retellings and scripts are read aloud by the teacher or in parts, and a student's
164:   lines are learned by ear and by the card. English learners: each unit teaches its six to ten words in depth, with a
165:   picture, a gesture or a sound and a frame ("My character wants ___ because ___." "The light changes when ___."),
166:   a student may show or act the word instead of saying it, and the check marks what the student performed apart from
167:   what they wrote or said.
168: - **Safety read.** Every unit is hands-on (movement on a shared floor; prop, costume-piece, set-model and light
169:   work), so every unit gets the fresh safety agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md) against free
170:   guides, named here and opened by the agent: the federal consumer product safety agency's "Art and Craft Safety
171:   Guide" (cpsc.gov) and California's environmental health hazard office's guidance on art and craft materials for
172:   schools, with its list of materials not to buy for grades K to 6 (oehha.ca.gov), for every making day; and the same
173:   federal agency's button battery safety pages (cpsc.gov), for the flashlights. Rules for every unit, from APPROACH
174:   "Safe use" and marked "from memory" until that read checks them: a clear floor with room for arms out, chairs and
175:   bags to the walls; bare feet or soft soles on a clean floor; no lifts, no falls rehearsed, no contact stage fights
176:   and no real or toy weapons (a sword is mimed or is a rolled paper tube held still); partner contact only by
177:   agreement and never required; voice rest and no forced loud sound; props checked by the teacher before use (no
178:   sharp edges, nothing small enough to swallow, no cords long enough to wrap a neck); no standing on chairs or
179:   tables; flashlights LED only, with screw-closed battery doors and no coin or button batteries; no light shone into
180:   eyes and no flashing or strobe; supplies labeled non-toxic; blades used by an adult only; no heat source; hands
181:   washed after glue and found objects. Each unit's last column gives its hazards and controls in the words days.csv
182:   will copy. No student sign-off form.
183: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md),
184:   which covers the works with the rest of the unit; no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Units with a
